Toward Socially Intelligent Human-Robot Collaboration in Dynamic Shared Spaces: A Unified Framework for Perception, Trust, and Adaptive Behavior

Ouadi Maakoul

Abstract

We present a mathematically rigorous, architecturally modular, and algorithmically tractable framework for socially intelligent human-robot collaboration (HRC) in dynamic shared environments. Building on an Extended Social Mini-Game Partially Observable Stochastic Game (eSMG-POSG) formulation that extends the SMG formalism of Chandra et al. (2025), we derive closed-form equations for adaptive proxemics via golden-ratio anisotropic Gaussian cost fields, multidimensional Bayesian trust dynamics via independent Beta conjugate updates, and particle-filtered intent inference with bounded-depth Theory of Mind. The Embodied Social Foundation Model (ESFM) integrates these components into a three-layer hierarchical architecture (reactive <10 ms, social 10–200 ms, relational >200 ms) with provable value approximation bounds under partial observability and a privacy-preserving perception design. We provide explicit algorithms for local policy computation via decentralized bi-level optimization with control barrier functions, Model Predictive Control with adaptive social cost fields, and variational preference learning for embodied RLHF. Theoretical analysis establishes convergence of multidimensional trust estimates, a bounded value error under local coupling assumptions, and computational complexity independent of global state space size. A novel proposition links trust in integrity to the weight of second-order belief updates. We also provide a formal likelihood model for cultural prior inference and a detailed roadmap for bridging the gap from theory to physical deployment. This work provides a formal, provably approximable foundation for trustworthy and culturally adaptive collaborative robots, with all proofs and algorithmic guarantees presented in full.

Keywords: Human-Robot Interaction, Dec-POMDP, Social Navigation, Proxemics, Bayesian Trust, Particle Filtering, Model Predictive Control, Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback, Formal Verification, Embodied AI

