1. Introduction

As autonomous robots transition from isolated industrial workcells to dynamic environments shared with humans, the ability to navigate and collaborate in a socially intelligent manner becomes paramount. A socially competent robot must not only avoid collisions but also respect personal space, anticipate human intent, calibrate its behavior based on earned trust, and adapt to cultural norms—all while operating under strict real-time constraints and partial observability.

The challenges of socially-aware navigation and human-robot collaboration have been approached from multiple directions. On one hand, formal methods based on Partially Observable Markov Decision Processes (POMDPs) and their multi-agent extensions (Dec-POMDPs, POSGs) provide a principled framework for decision-making under uncertainty [Oliehoek & Amato, 2016]. However, the computational intractability of exact solutions in continuous state-action spaces has limited their direct application to real-time social navigation. On the other hand, heuristic approaches such as the Social Force Model [Helbing & Molnár, 1995] and reciprocal collision avoidance (ORCA) [van den Berg et al., 2008] offer computational efficiency but lack formal guarantees and the ability to reason about latent human mental states such as intent, trust, and beliefs.

Recent work has sought to bridge this divide. Chandra et al. (2025) introduced Social Mini-Games (SMGs)—a formalism that decomposes multi-agent social navigation into local, temporally bounded interactions, enabling tractable decentralized planning with provable safety via Control Barrier Functions (CBFs). Spurný et al. (2025) proposed a dynamic proxemic model based on the golden ratio, demonstrating that anisotropic, velocity-dependent personal space representations better align with human comfort expectations. In parallel, Bayesian models of trust have emerged as a robust method for online estimation of human-robot trust dynamics from observable interaction signals [Hancock et al., 2011; Chauhan et al., 2025].

In this paper, we present a unified theoretical framework that integrates these threads into a single, mathematically grounded architecture. Our contributions are threefold:

1. Extended SMG-POSG Formulation: We extend the SMG formalism of Chandra et al. (2025) by explicitly defining a coupling set via a novel liveness function, integrating LTL/PCTL specifications for formal safety verification, and providing a packing bound (Lemma 2.1) that guarantees the size of the local interaction subgame is independent of the total number of agents in the environment.
2. Integrated Trust and Theory of Mind: We combine multidimensional Bayesian trust dynamics (modeled via independent Beta distributions for competence, benevolence, and integrity) with particle-filtered, bounded-depth Social Theory of Mind (S-ToM). A key theoretical innovation is Proposition 2.3, which establishes that the robot's trust in a human's integrity directly modulates the weight given to second-order belief estimates, providing a principled mechanism for trust-calibrated social reasoning.
3. Provably Approximable Architecture: We provide a complete theoretical analysis of the Embodied Social Foundation Model (ESFM). This includes a closed-form Lipschitz constant for the golden-ratio anisotropic Gaussian cost field (Corollary 2.1), a full proof of the value approximation bound under local coupling (Theorem 2.1), and worst-case complexity guarantees for the three-layer hierarchical architecture (Theorem 4.1). We also present a formal likelihood model for inferring cultural priors from proxemic observations.

This paper is structured as a theoretical framework contribution. Section 2 establishes the mathematical foundations, including all definitions, lemmas, theorems, and complete proofs. Section 3 describes the ESFM architecture and its privacy-preserving perception pipeline. Section 4 provides the algorithmic specifications and complexity analysis. Section 5 outlines a proposed evaluation protocol for future empirical validation. Section 6 consolidates the theoretical guarantees, and Section 7 discusses limitations and provides a detailed roadmap for bridging the gap from theory to physical deployment. All proofs are included in Appendix B.

By providing a unified, provably approximable foundation, this work aims to serve as a rigorous theoretical underpinning for the next generation of socially intelligent robots—bridging formal methods, embodied AI, and social cognition.

Here are Chapters 2, 3, 4, and 5 of the manuscript, incorporating all critical fixes: complete proofs, the trust–Theory of Mind connection (Proposition 2.3), a formal cultural prior likelihood model, explicit complexity theorems, and a detailed proposed evaluation protocol.

