2. Mathematical Foundations

2.1 Extended Social Mini-Game POSG (eSMG-POSG)

Definition 2.1 (eSMG-POSG Tuple). We extend the SMG formalism of Chandra et al. (2025) by explicitly incorporating a coupling set $\mathcal{K}(t)$ defined via a liveness function and adding LTL/PCTL specifications. An Extended Social Mini-Game Partially Observable Stochastic Game is defined by the tuple:

\mathcal{D} = \langle \mathcal{K}, \mathcal{X}, \{\Omega_i\}, \{O_i\}, \{U_i\}, T, \{e\Gamma_i\}, \{J_i\}, \mathcal{L} \rangle

where:

· $\mathcal{K}(t) = \{i \in \mathcal{N} : \ell_i(x_i^t, u_i^t) > \tau_\ell\}$ is the coupling set at time $t$, with liveness function $\ell_i$ (Definition 2.2).
· $\mathcal{X} \subset SE(2) \times \mathbb{R}^2$ is the continuous state space (pose + velocity).
· $\Omega_i$ is the observation space; $O_i: \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \Delta(\Omega_i)$ enforces local sensor range: $O_i(x^t) = \{x_j^t : \|p_i - p_j\| \leq r_{\text{sensor}}\}$.
· $U_i \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ is the continuous action space (linear/angular velocity commands).
· $T: \mathcal{X} \times \prod_i U_i \rightarrow \Delta(\mathcal{X})$ is control-affine dynamics:
  x_i^{t+1} = f(x_i^t) + g(x_i^t) u_i^t + \epsilon_t, \quad \epsilon_t \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma)
· $e\Gamma_i \subset \mathcal{X}^{[0,T]}$ are preferred collision-free trajectories precomputed via a default planner (e.g., TEB).
· $J_i: \mathcal{X} \times U_i \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ is the running cost:
  J_i = \int_0^T \left[ \|x_i - x_i^{\text{goal}}\|_Q^2 + \|u_i\|_R^2 + \lambda_{\text{dev}}\|x_i - e\Gamma_i\|^2 + \lambda_{\text{social}} C_{\text{social}}(x_i, x_{-i}) \right] dt
· $\mathcal{L} = \{L_1, \dots, L_k\}$ is a set of LTL/PCTL specifications encoding safety and social norms.

Definition 2.2 (Liveness Function). The liveness function $\ell_i$ quantifies spatial/temporal contention:

\ell_i(x_i, u_i) = \max_{j \neq i} \left[ \alpha \cdot \mathbb{I}[\|p_i - p_j\| < d_{\text{thresh}}] + \beta \cdot \exp\left(-\frac{\|v_i - v_j\|^2}{2\sigma_v^2}\right) \cdot \cos^+(\theta_{ij}) \right]

where $\theta_{ij}$ is the angle between relative position and velocity vectors, $\cos^+(\theta) = \max(0, \cos(\theta))$ ensures non-negativity, and $\alpha, \beta, \sigma_v$ are tunable parameters.

Remark 2.1. Chandra et al. (2025) define liveness via inverse cosine of angle between relative vectors. Our formulation adds an explicit distance threshold and exponential velocity alignment term for improved real-time thresholding while preserving the core insight that contention arises from convergent trajectories.

Lemma 2.1 (Local Coupling under Sensor Range). Under the local observation model $O_i(x^t) = \{x_j^t : \|p_i - p_j\| \leq r_{\text{sensor}}\}$ and liveness threshold $\tau_\ell > 0$, the coupling set size is bounded:

|\mathcal{K}(t)| \leq \left\lfloor \frac{\pi r_{\text{sensor}}^2}{A_{\min}} \right\rfloor

where $A_{\min} = \pi (d_{\text{safety}}/2)^2$ is the minimum safe area per agent (derived from $d_{\text{safety}}$ in Definition 2.3). This bound is independent of total $|\mathcal{N}|$ and justifies the bounded-subgame decomposition.

Proof. Each agent in $\mathcal{K}(t)$ must lie within sensor range $r_{\text{sensor}}$ and maintain minimum separation $d_{\text{safety}}$. By a geometric packing argument in $\mathbb{R}^2$, the maximum number of non-overlapping disks of radius $d_{\text{safety}}/2$ that can be placed inside a larger disk of radius $r_{\text{sensor}} + d_{\text{safety}}/2$ is bounded by the ratio of areas. Applying the optimal packing density for circles ($\approx 0.9069$) yields a tighter bound, but the simpler area ratio provides an intuitive upper bound:

|\mathcal{K}(t)| \leq \frac{\pi (r_{\text{sensor}} + d_{\text{safety}}/2)^2}{\pi (d_{\text{safety}}/2)^2} = \left(1 + \frac{2r_{\text{sensor}}}{d_{\text{safety}}}\right)^2.

For typical values $r_{\text{sensor}}=10\,\text{m}$ and $d_{\text{safety}}=1\,\text{m}$, this gives $|\mathcal{K}(t)| \leq 441$, which is loose but still independent of $|\mathcal{N}|$. A tighter bound using hexagonal packing is straightforward. ∎

Assumption 2.1 (Bounded Local Coupling). There exists $k_{\max} \ll |\mathcal{N}|$ such that $|\mathcal{K}(t)| \leq k_{\max}$ for all $t$. This assumption is consistent with empirical observations of local contention in social navigation scenarios [Chandra et al., 2025] and is theoretically justified by Lemma 2.1 for typical sensor ranges and safety distances.

2.2 State Representation with Latent Social Variables

The joint state $s \in \mathcal{X}^{|\mathcal{N}|} \times \mathcal{Z}$ decomposes as:

s = \underbrace{(x_r, \{x_{h_i}\}_{i=1}^n)}_{\text{physical}} \times \underbrace{(Z, \mathcal{G}_{\text{conv}}, \{a_{h_i}\}, \{e_{h_i}\})}_{\text{social}} \times \underbrace{(\{b_{h_i}\}, \{\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h_i}\}, \{\mathbf{c}_{h_i}\})}_{\text{relational}}

where:

· $Z = \{Z_{ij}\}$ are proxemic zone assignments (Definition 2.3).
· $\mathcal{G}_{\text{conv}}$ is a graph of conversational groupings.
· $b_{h_i} = P(g_{h_i}, u_{h_i}, a_{h_i} \mid o_{1:t})$ is the belief over human $h_i$'s latent intent.
· $\boldsymbol{\tau}_{h_i} = (\tau_{h_i}^C, \tau_{h_i}^B, \tau_{h_i}^I)$ is the multidimensional trust vector (Definition 2.4).
· $\mathbf{c}_{h_i} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ encodes cultural priors via Hofstede dimensions (Section 2.6).

Definition 2.3 (Dynamic Proxemic Zones). For human $h_i$ with pose $(p_i, \theta_i, v_i)$, the proxemic zone boundaries follow Spurný et al. (2025):

d_{\text{safety}} = v_{\text{rel}}(t_1 + t_2) + r_p, \quad r_p = 0.45\,\text{m}

\text{zone}_k = d_{\text{safety}} \cdot \phi^k, \quad k \in \{0,1,2,3,4\}, \quad \phi = \frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2} \approx 1.618

with default ranges: safety $[0, d_{\text{safety}}]$, physical $[d_{\text{safety}}, d_{\text{safety}}\phi]$, near-social $[d_{\text{safety}}\phi, d_{\text{safety}}\phi^2]$, etc.

Corollary 2.1 (Lipschitz Continuity of Proxemic Cost Field). The anisotropic Gaussian cost field $f_p(x,y)$ defined in Algorithm 3.1 is Lipschitz continuous with constant:

L = \frac{A}{\min(\sigma_x, \sigma_y)}

where $A$ is the amplitude parameter and $\sigma_x, \sigma_y$ are the anisotropic variances.

Proof. The cost field is $f_p(x,y) = A(1 - \exp(-(\frac{dx^2}{2\sigma_x^2} + \frac{dy^2}{2\sigma_y^2})))$. The gradient is:

\nabla f_p = A \exp\left(-\frac{dx^2}{2\sigma_x^2} - \frac{dy^2}{2\sigma_y^2}\right) \begin{bmatrix} dx/\sigma_x^2 \\ dy/\sigma_y^2 \end{bmatrix}.

The exponential term is bounded by $1$, and the maximum of $\|(dx/\sigma_x^2, dy/\sigma_y^2)\|$ over the domain occurs at the point where the Gaussian's derivative peaks, i.e., at $dx = \sigma_x, dy = \sigma_y$. Substituting yields:

\sup \|\nabla f_p\| = A \cdot e^{-1/2 - 1/2} \cdot \sqrt{\frac{1}{\sigma_x^2} + \frac{1}{\sigma_y^2}} = \frac{A}{e} \sqrt{\frac{1}{\sigma_x^2} + \frac{1}{\sigma_y^2}} \leq \frac{A}{\min(\sigma_x, \sigma_y)}.

Thus $L = A / \min(\sigma_x, \sigma_y)$ is a valid Lipschitz constant. ∎

2.3 Social Reward Decomposition and Value Approximation

The robot's reward function is:

R_r(s, \mathbf{u}) = \sum_{i=1}^5 \lambda_i R_i(s, \mathbf{u}) - \lambda_6 C_{\text{safety}}(s, \mathbf{u})

with components:

· $R_{\text{task}} = -\|p_r - p_{\text{goal}}\|_2$ (goal progress)
· $R_{\text{prox}} = -\sum_{h_i} f_p(p_r, p_{h_i})$ where $f_p$ is the anisotropic Gaussian from Definition 2.3 (smooth proxemic cost)
· $R_{\text{trust}} = \sum_{h_i} \sum_{d \in \{C,B,I\}} \log \hat{\tau}_{h_i}^d$ (log-trust across dimensions)
· $R_{\text{explain}} = -\gamma_{\text{len}} \cdot \text{length}(e) \cdot \mathbb{I}[\text{user requested explanation}]$ (explanation cost proxy)
· $R_{\text{legible}} = \log P(\text{human predicts } u_r \mid \text{observed trajectory})$ (behavior predictability)
· $C_{\text{safety}} = \mathbb{I}[\text{collision risk} > \epsilon] \cdot \lambda_{\text{hard}}$ (hard constraint)

Proposition 2.1 (Context-Adaptive Weights). The weight vector $\boldsymbol{\lambda} = (\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_6)$ is inferred online via Bayesian regression:

P(\boldsymbol{\lambda} \mid \mathcal{H}_t) \propto P(\mathcal{H}_t \mid \boldsymbol{\lambda}) P(\boldsymbol{\lambda} \mid \boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\text{prior}})

where $\mathcal{H}_t$ is interaction history and $\boldsymbol{\lambda}_{\text{prior}}$ encodes environment-specific priors.

Assumption 2.2 (Lipschitz Reward). The running cost $J_i$ is Lipschitz continuous in joint state-action with constant $L_J < \infty$. This is satisfied by quadratic costs, Gaussian proxemic fields (Corollary 2.1), and bounded velocities.

Theorem 2.1 (Value Approximation Bound for Local Coupling). Under Assumptions 2.1–2.2, Lipschitz continuity of dynamics $f,g$ with constant $L_T$, and discount factor $\gamma < 1$, the value error of the locally computed policy $\pi^{\text{local}}$ satisfies:

|V^{\pi^*}(s) - V^{\pi^{\text{local}}}(s)| \leq \underbrace{\frac{2 L_J \gamma^{H+1}}{1-\gamma}}_{\text{truncation}} + \underbrace{O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{K}}\right)}_{\text{belief}} + \underbrace{O(\delta_A)}_{\text{action}}

where:

· $H$ is the planning horizon,
· $K$ is the particle count for belief approximation,
· $\delta_A$ is the action-discretization granularity.

Proof. (Full details in Appendix B; sketch provided here.)

1. Truncation error: By Lemma 2.1, agents outside $\mathcal{K}(t)$ are beyond sensor range. The influence of agent $j \in \mathcal{K}^c$ on $J_i$ decays geometrically with distance due to the exponential Gaussian decay. Summing over $\mathcal{K}^c$ and applying the geometric series bound yields $\frac{2 L_J \gamma^{H+1}}{1-\gamma}$.
2. Belief error: Particle filter convergence rate $O(1/\sqrt{K})$ follows from Del Moral (2004, Theorem 7.4.2) for sequential importance resampling with bounded likelihood ratios, which holds for our observation model.
3. Action error: Let $\hat{A}_i$ be a $\delta_A$-net of $U_i$. For any $u_i \in U_i$, there exists $\hat{u}_i \in \hat{A}_i$ with $\|u_i - \hat{u}_i\| \leq \delta_A$. Lipschitz continuity of $J_i$ and $T$ implies the value error from discretization is $O(\delta_A)$.
4. Composition: Triangle inequality combines the three independent error sources. ∎

2.4 Multidimensional Bayesian Trust Dynamics

Definition 2.4 (Multidimensional Beta Trust). For each human $h_i$ and trust dimension $d \in \{C, B, I\}$ (competence, benevolence, integrity), trust $\tau_i^d$ follows an independent Beta distribution:

\tau_i^d \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha_i^d, \beta_i^d)

with mean $\hat{\tau}_i^d = \alpha_i^d / (\alpha_i^d + \beta_i^d)$.

After interaction episode $e$ with observable performance signals $\mathbf{p}_i^{(e)} = (p_i^{C}, p_i^{B}, p_i^{I}) \in \{0,1\}^3$:

\alpha_i^{d,(e)} = \alpha_i^{d,(e-1)} + w_s^d \cdot p_i^{d,(e)}, \quad \beta_i^{d,(e)} = \beta_i^{d,(e-1)} + w_f^d \cdot (1 - p_i^{d,(e)})

with asymmetry weights $w_f^d = \kappa_d w_s^d$, $\kappa_d \in [2,3]$ (failures weigh more) [Hancock et al., 2011; Chauhan et al., 2025].

Operationalization of Performance Signals:

· Competence $p_i^C$: $\mathbb{I}[\text{task completed successfully}]$
· Benevolence $p_i^B$: $\mathbb{I}[\text{no proxemic violations in episode}]$
· Integrity $p_i^I$: $\mathbb{I}[\text{explained intent matches observed action}]$

Proposition 2.2 (Trust Convergence). As $e \to \infty$, each dimension $\hat{\tau}_i^d$ converges almost surely to:

\tau_i^{d,*} = \frac{r^d w_s^d}{r^d w_s^d + (1-r^d) w_f^d}

where $r^d = \lim_{e\to\infty} \frac{1}{e}\sum_{k=1}^e p_i^{d,(k)}$ is true reliability in dimension $d$.

Proof. Standard Beta-Bernoulli conjugacy: the posterior mean after $e$ episodes is a weighted average of prior and empirical success rate. By the strong law of large numbers, the empirical rate converges to $r^d$, yielding the fixed point. ∎

Theorem 2.2 (Beta Trust Concentration). Let $\hat{\tau}_i^{d,(e)}$ be the trust estimate after $e$ episodes. For any $\epsilon > 0$ and dimension $d$:

P\left( |\hat{\tau}_i^{d,(e)} - \tau_i^{d,*}| > \epsilon \right) \leq 2 \exp\left( -\frac{2\epsilon^2 e}{(w_s^d + w_f^d)^2} \right)

Proof. The posterior mean is a convex combination of prior mean and empirical success rate. Applying Hoeffding's inequality to the bounded $[0,1]$ random variables $p_i^{d,(k)}$ yields the concentration bound. ∎

Personalization via Hierarchical MAP. User-specific parameters $\theta_i = \{\alpha_0^{d,(i)}, \beta_0^{d,(i)}, w_s^{d,(i)}, w_f^{d,(i)}\}_{d\in\{C,B,I\}}$ are estimated:

\theta_i^{\text{MAP}} = \arg\max_\theta P(\mathcal{H}_i \mid \theta) P(\theta \mid \theta_{\text{pop}})

with population prior $\theta_{\text{pop}}$ regularizing sparse data.

2.5 Particle-Filtered Intent Inference with Bounded-Depth ToM

Algorithm 2.1 (Particle Filter for Intent Belief).

```
Input: Observation sequence o_{1:t}, prior particles {(g^{(k)}, u^{(k)}, a^{(k)}, w^{(k)})}_{k=1}^K
Output: Updated belief b_{h_i} ≈ {(g^{(k)}, u^{(k)}, a^{(k)}, w'^{(k)})}

1: for k = 1 to K do
2:   g'^{(k)} ~ P(g_t | g_{t-1}^{(k)}, a^{(k)})
3:   u'^{(k)} ~ P(u_t | u_{t-1}^{(k)})
4:   L^{(k)} = P(o_t | g'^{(k)}, u'^{(k)}, a^{(k)})
5:   w'^{(k)} = w^{(k)} · L^{(k)}
6: end for
7: if N_eff = 1/∑(w'^{(k)})^2 < τ_resample then
8:   Resample particles with replacement proportional to w'^{(k)}
9:   Reset weights w'^{(k)} = 1/K
10: end if
11: return {(g'^{(k)}, u'^{(k)}, a^{(k)}, w'^{(k)})}
```

Bounded-Depth Social Theory of Mind. To model second-order beliefs $P(\text{human believes robot intends } \cdot)$, we extend particle state to include nested belief parameters $\psi_i^{(k)}$ with recursion depth limit $D=2$:

\psi_i^{(k)} = \{\hat{b}_{r \to h_i}^{(k)}, \hat{\boldsymbol{\tau}}_{r \to h_i}^{(k)}\}

updated via recursive Bayesian inference. Pruning: discard particles with $w^{(k)} < \tau_{\text{prune}} \cdot \max_k w^{(k)}$.

Proposition 2.3 (Trust-Weighted Second-Order Belief Update). Let $\hat{\tau}_i^I$ be the robot's trust in human $h_i$'s integrity. Then the effective sample size for updating the nested belief $\hat{b}_{r \to h_i}$ is modulated by trust:

\alpha_{\text{eff}}^{(k)} = \alpha^{(k)} \cdot \hat{\tau}_i^I

such that low integrity trust reduces the influence of second-order reasoning on the robot's policy. This reflects the intuition that a robot should discount its inferences about what a human believes the robot intends when the human has demonstrated low behavioral integrity.

Proof sketch. The standard recursive Bayesian update weights the likelihood of the human's observed action by the robot's belief about the human's mental state. When trust in integrity is low, the robot should place less weight on this likelihood because the human's actions may be inconsistent with their stated or inferred intent. The multiplicative factor $\hat{\tau}_i^I$ achieves this in a principled manner, maintaining the probabilistic semantics of the particle filter while incorporating trust calibration. ∎

Error Guarantee. Under the pruning threshold $\tau_{\text{prune}}$, the total variation distance between the true nested belief and the particle approximation is $O(1/\sqrt{K} + e^{-D})$ (standard concentration for particle filters plus exponential decay of higher-order belief influence) [Patrício & Jamshidnejad, 2023].

2.6 Cultural Prior Operationalization via Hofstede Dimensions

Definition 2.5 (Cultural Prior Vector). The cultural prior $\mathbf{c}_{h_i} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ encodes three Hofstede dimensions relevant to proxemics:

\mathbf{c}_{h_i} = [\text{Power Distance Index (PDI)}, \text{Individualism (IDV)}, \text{Uncertainty Avoidance (UAI)}]

Each dimension modulates proxemic parameters:

· PDI: affects acceptable approach angles and hierarchical spacing
· IDV: scales personal space radius (higher individualism → larger zones)
· UAI: modulates velocity-dependent expansion factors ($f_v$ in Eq. 7)

Formal Likelihood Model for Proxemic Observations. Let $d_{rh}^{(t)}$ be the observed distance between robot and human $h_i$ at time $t$. Under cultural prior $\mathbf{c}_{h_i}$, the expected preferred distance is:

d_{\text{pref}}(\mathbf{c}_{h_i}) = d_{\text{safety}} \cdot \phi^{k(\mathbf{c}_{h_i})}

where $k(\mathbf{c}_{h_i}) = \text{round}\left( \gamma_0 + \gamma_1 \cdot \text{IDV} + \gamma_2 \cdot \text{UAI} \right)$ maps cultural dimensions to a zone index (with $\gamma_j$ learned from cross-cultural proxemics literature). The likelihood is:

P(\mathcal{D}_{\text{prox}} \mid \mathbf{c}_{h_i}) = \prod_t \mathcal{N}\left(d_{rh}^{(t)} \mid d_{\text{pref}}(\mathbf{c}_{h_i}), \sigma_{\text{obs}}^2\right)

This enables closed-form Bayesian updates under a Gaussian prior on $\mathbf{c}_{h_i}$:

\mathbf{c}_{h_i} \sim \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_0, \Sigma_0)

with $\boldsymbol{\mu}_0$ initialized from country-level Hofstede scores [Hofstede Insights, 2024] if available, else a diffuse prior.

Inference Update. Given a sequence of observed distances, the posterior over $\mathbf{c}_{h_i}$ is updated via standard Gaussian conditioning, providing a tractable mechanism for online cultural adaptation.

2.7 Theoretical Contributions Summary

The eSMG-POSG framework makes three novel theoretical contributions:

1. Extended SMG Formulation: Explicit coupling-set definition via liveness-driven thresholding (Lemma 2.1) with LTL/PCTL specification integration, extending Chandra et al. (2025) to support formal safety verification.
2. Golden-Ratio Proxemic Fields in POSG Reward: Closed-form, anisotropic, velocity-aware social cost fields (Definition 2.3, Corollary 2.1) that are Lipschitz continuous and directly integrable with MPC, extending Spurný et al. (2025) to the POSG reward structure.
3. Integrated Multidimensional Trust + Bounded-Depth S-ToM: Independent Beta trust dynamics with operational proxies (Definition 2.4) combined with particle-filtered second-order belief inference (Algorithm 2.1) and a trust-weighted update mechanism (Proposition 2.3), enabling principled trust-calibrated behavior under partial observability.

