4. Algorithms

4.1 Local eSMG-POSG Solver via Decentralized Bi-Level Optimization

Algorithm 4.1 (Bi-Level Optimization for Social Mini-Games).

```
Input: Coupling set K, particle beliefs {b_i}, horizon H
Output: Collision-free trajectories {ξ_i} for agents in K

// TOP LEVEL: Fair ordering via combinatorial assignment
1: Construct contention graph G_c = (K, E_c) where (i,j) ∈ E_c if ℓ_i, ℓ_j > τ_ℓ
2: Solve minimum-cost maximum-flow to assign priority orderings π: K → {1,...,|K|}
3: for each agent i ∈ K do
4:   // BOTTOM LEVEL: Individual trajectory optimization
5:   ξ_i* = argmin_{ξ_i} [J_i(ξ_i) + λ_social · C_social(ξ_i, {ξ_j}_{j<i})]
6:   subject to: dynamics constraints, CBF safety constraints
7:   // CBF ensures forward invariance of safe set
8:   h(x_i, x_j) ≥ 0 → ḣ(x_i, x_j) + α(h(x_i, x_j)) ≥ 0
9: end for
10: return {ξ_i*}
```

Theorem 4.1 (Layer-wise Worst-Case Complexity). Under Assumption 2.1 with $k_{\max}$ bound, and given $n$ total obstacles in sensor range, $K$ particles, and MPC horizon $H$, the per-timestep computational complexity is:

· Reactive layer: $O(n \log n)$
· Social layer: $O(k_{\max}^3 + k_{\max} \cdot H^3 + k_{\max} \cdot K \log K)$
· Relational layer: $O(1)$

Proof.

· Reactive: ORCA runs in $O(n \log n)$ using spatial partitioning [van den Berg et al., 2011].
· Social: Top-level assignment via Hungarian algorithm is $O(k_{\max}^3)$. Each of $k_{\max}$ MPC solves uses interior-point methods with complexity $O(H^3)$ for the Newton step. Particle filter resampling (systematic) is $O(K \log K)$ per human, and $|K| \leq k_{\max}$.
· Relational: Beta updates and cultural prior conditioning are constant-time operations per human.

Thus total complexity is $O(n \log n + k_{\max}^3 + k_{\max} H^3 + k_{\max} K \log K)$. ∎

Corollary 4.1 (Scalability). The social-layer complexity is independent of global agent count $|\mathcal{N}|$, depending only on the bounded coupling set size $k_{\max}$ and planning horizon $H$. This enables deployment in crowded environments where $|\mathcal{N}| \gg k_{\max}$.

4.2 Variational Preference Learning for Embodied RLHF

Objective. Learn reward function $R_\psi(s,a)$ from human trajectory preferences $\mathcal{D}_{\text{pref}} = \{(\tau^{(1)}, \tau^{(2)}, y)\}$.

Variational Bound [Poddar et al., 2024]:

\log P(y=1 \mid \tau^{(1)}, \tau^{(2)}) \geq \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi(z)}[\log P(y \mid z, \tau^{(1)}, \tau^{(2)})] - \text{KL}(q_\phi(z) \| p(z))

Algorithm 4.2 (Trajectory Preference Optimization).

```
Input: Preference dataset D_pref, policy π_θ, reward model R_ψ
1: while not converged do
2:   τ^{(1)}, τ^{(2)} ~ π_θ(·|s_0)
3:   z = Encoder(τ^{(1)}, τ^{(2)})  // shared encoder
4:   ΔR_ψ = Σ_t [R_ψ(s_t^{(1)}, a_t^{(1)}) - R_ψ(s_t^{(2)}, a_t^{(2)})]
5:   L = -[y·log σ(ΔR_ψ) + (1-y)·log(1-σ(ΔR_ψ))] + β·KL(q_φ(z)||p(z))
6:   ψ ← ψ - η_ψ ∇_ψ L
7:   θ ← θ + η_θ ∇_θ 𝔼_{τ~π_θ}[Σ_t R_ψ(s_t, a_t)]  // PPO-style
8: end while
9: return π_θ, R_ψ
```

Personalization Extension. User-specific reward weights $\lambda_i^{(u)}$ are inferred via a hierarchical Bayesian model:

\lambda_i^{(u)} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_i^{\text{pop}}, \sigma_i^{\text{pop}}), \quad \mu_i^{\text{pop}}, \sigma_i^{\text{pop}} \sim \text{Hyperprior}

This allows the learned reward function to adapt to individual preferences while regularizing toward population-level behavior.

