8. Conclusion

We have presented the Embodied Social Foundation Model (ESFM) —a mathematically rigorous, architecturally modular, and algorithmically tractable framework for socially intelligent human-robot collaboration. The work makes three primary theoretical contributions.

First, we extended the Social Mini-Game formalism of Chandra et al. (2025) into an eSMG-POSG formulation with an explicit coupling set defined via a novel liveness function, a packing bound (Lemma 2.1) guaranteeing bounded local subgames, and integration of LTL/PCTL specifications for formal safety verification.

Second, we integrated multidimensional Bayesian trust dynamics (independent Beta models for competence, benevolence, and integrity) with particle-filtered bounded-depth Theory of Mind. A key insight, formalized in Proposition 2.3, is that trust in integrity directly modulates the weight given to second-order belief estimates, enabling principled trust-calibrated social reasoning.

Third, we provided a complete theoretical analysis of the three-layer hierarchical architecture. This includes a closed-form Lipschitz constant for the golden-ratio anisotropic Gaussian proxemic cost field (Corollary 2.1), a full proof of the value approximation bound under local coupling (Theorem 2.1), and worst-case complexity guarantees (Theorem 4.1) showing that the social-layer computation is independent of the total number of agents in the environment.

The ESFM framework bridges formal methods, embodied AI, and social cognition. By providing a unified, provably approximable foundation, this work aims to serve as a rigorous theoretical underpinning for the next generation of socially intelligent robots—robots that can navigate shared spaces with an understanding of personal space, trust, intent, and cultural context.

