Appendix F: Mathematical Background

F.1 Partially Observable Stochastic Games (POSG)

A POSG extends Markov games to partial observability. Formally, $\langle \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{S}, \{\mathcal{A}_i\}, \{\Omega_i\}, \{O_i\}, T, \{R_i\}, \gamma \rangle$ where:

· $\mathcal{I}$ is the set of agents.
· $\mathcal{S}$ is the state space.
· $\mathcal{A}_i$ is agent $i$'s action space.
· $\Omega_i$ is agent $i$'s observation space.
· $O_i(s, a, o) = P(o | s, a)$ is the observation model.
· $T(s' | s, \mathbf{a})$ is the transition function.
· $R_i(s, \mathbf{a})$ is the reward function.
· $\gamma$ is the discount factor.

The goal is to find a joint policy $\pi = (\pi_1, \dots, \pi_n)$ that maximizes expected cumulative reward. Exact solutions are NEXP-complete; approximations are necessary.

F.2 Control Barrier Functions (CBF)

For a control-affine system $\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x)u$, a function $h(x)$ defines a safe set $\mathcal{C} = \{x : h(x) \geq 0\}$. $h$ is a CBF if there exists a class $\mathcal{K}$ function $\alpha$ such that:

\sup_u [L_f h(x) + L_g h(x) u + \alpha(h(x))] \geq 0

Any Lipschitz continuous controller satisfying this inequality renders $\mathcal{C}$ forward invariant.

F.3 Beta-Bernoulli Conjugacy

If prior $\tau \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha, \beta)$ and observations $x_i \sim \text{Bernoulli}(\tau)$, then posterior $\tau | x_{1:n} \sim \text{Beta}(\alpha + \sum x_i, \beta + \sum (1-x_i))$. The mean is $\frac{\alpha + \sum x_i}{\alpha + \beta + n}$.

F.4 Particle Filter Convergence

Under mild regularity conditions, the empirical distribution of a particle filter converges to the true filtering distribution at rate $O(1/\sqrt{K})$ in $L_1$ norm, where $K$ is the number of particles [Del Moral, 2004].

F.5 Hofstede Cultural Dimensions

· Power Distance Index (PDI): Extent to which less powerful members accept unequal power distribution.
· Individualism (IDV): Preference for loosely-knit social framework vs. collectivism.
· Uncertainty Avoidance (UAI): Degree of comfort with ambiguity and uncertainty.

Scores are available for most countries (0-100 scale) [Hofstede Insights, 2024].

F.6 Model Predictive Control (MPC)

MPC solves a finite-horizon optimal control problem at each timestep:

\min_{u_{0:H-1}} \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \ell(x_k, u_k) + V_f(x_H) \quad \text{s.t. } x_{k+1} = f(x_k, u_k), \; x_k \in \mathcal{X}, u_k \in \mathcal{U}

Only the first control $u_0$ is applied; then the horizon shifts forward.

